  title="Fall Gutter Cleaning in Middle Tennessee, and What Skipping It Costs"
  description="Clogged gutters rot fascia, back water under shingles, and soak crawlspaces. When to clear them in Middle Tennessee and what to check while you are up there."
>
<p>Clogged gutters are not a curb appeal problem. They are a water management failure, and in Middle Tennessee the damage shows up in a predictable order: rotted fascia and soffit first, then saturated soil against the foundation, then a wet crawlspace, then the expensive things that live in a wet crawlspace. A gutter cleaning is one of the cheapest line items on any property. The repairs it prevents are among the most expensive.</p>

<p>Most Middle Tennessee properties need gutters cleared twice a year, and the more important of the two is after the leaves finish dropping, which around here generally runs from mid October into late November depending on the tree mix. Here is the mechanism, the timing, and what to actually check while someone is up there.</p>

<h2>What a gutter is actually doing</h2>

<p>A roof sheds a remarkable volume of water. A one inch rain on a 2,000 square foot roof footprint moves well over a thousand gallons, and the gutter system exists to collect all of it and put it somewhere away from the building. When it works, water runs off the roof, into the gutter, down the downspout, and out past the foundation.</p>

<p>When the gutter is blocked, that water does three things instead, all of them bad.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>It overflows the front edge,</strong> running down the fascia board and the siding below, which is what puts a stripe of rot in the wood trim.</li>
<li><strong>It backs up under the shingles</strong> at the roof edge, because a full gutter holds water above the drip edge. That is how a roof leak starts at the eave rather than in the field.</li>
<li><strong>It dumps in a concentrated line at the foundation,</strong> which is the one that costs the most.</li>
</ul>

<p>A blocked gutter also holds standing water and wet debris, which is heavy. Wet leaf pack and a few gallons of water is enough weight to pull spikes and hangers loose over a season, so the gutter starts sagging and pitching the wrong direction, which makes the next clog worse.</p>

<h2>Why this matters more in Middle Tennessee than most places</h2>

<p>Two local realities compound it. First, the tree cover. Mature oaks, maples, sweetgums, and pines are everywhere in our neighborhoods, and sweetgum balls and pine needles are particularly good at packing a downspout solid. Second, a large share of our housing stock sits on a crawlspace rather than a slab.</p>

<p>That second one is the important one. Water dumped against the foundation does not evaporate, it soaks in, and on a crawlspace house it finds its way under the building. The result is elevated humidity in the crawl, which leads to condensation on ductwork, damp insulation that falls out of the joists, wood rot in the framing, and the conditions that support mold and pests. Roof water that is not being carried away from the house is one of the most common sources of it, and the components that suffer, ductwork, insulation, framing, and the systems sitting in that space, are the ones with real replacement costs. Our reference on <a href="/articles/how-long-do-home-systems-last/">how long home systems actually last</a> is a useful reminder that moisture is what shortens most of those numbers.</p>

<p>The cost comparison is stark. Cleaning gutters is a routine maintenance visit. Replacing rotted fascia, re-grading, encapsulating a crawlspace, or repairing framing is a project. Same water, different amount of time ignoring it.</p>

<h2>When to do it</h2>

<p>Twice a year is the working standard for most properties here:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Late fall, after the leaves are down.</strong> This is the one that matters most. Cleaning in early October when half the leaves are still on the tree means doing it again in December, or heading into winter with a packed gutter.</li>
<li><strong>Spring, after the seed and bloom drop.</strong> Maple seeds, oak tassels, and pollen mats form a surprisingly effective plug, and spring is our wettest stretch.</li>
</ul>

<p>Properties under heavy tree cover often need three visits. Properties with no trees within fifty feet may get by with one. The honest way to find out is to check after the first hard rain following a clean and see what came back.</p>

<p>A note on winter here: a gutter full of wet debris that freezes turns into a solid block of ice hanging off the roof edge. It adds weight where the hangers are already loaded and it makes ice damming worse where it happens. Clearing the gutters before the first freeze is part of the same job as clearing them for the rain.</p>

<h2>What should be checked while someone is up there</h2>

<p>The cleaning is the reason for the visit. The inspection is most of the value. Anyone on a ladder at your roof edge should be looking at:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Downspouts flowing.</strong> Not just the trough being empty. Water has to actually come out the bottom. A clear gutter feeding a packed downspout is still a blocked system.</li>
<li><strong>Where the water goes at the bottom.</strong> Extensions or splash blocks should carry discharge several feet from the foundation. A downspout that ends at the corner of the house is a leak in slow motion. This is the single most commonly ignored item on the list and one of the cheapest to fix.</li>
<li><strong>Pitch and hangers.</strong> Standing water in a cleaned gutter means the pitch is wrong or a section has sagged.</li>
<li><strong>Fascia and soffit.</strong> Probe the wood behind the gutter. Soft fascia means this has been going on for a while, and repairing it now is far cheaper than after it spreads into the rafter tails.</li>
<li><strong>Seams and end caps.</strong> Sealant fails. A dripping seam puts water in exactly one spot, repeatedly.</li>
<li><strong>The roof edge itself.</strong> Granule loss, lifted shingles, and damaged drip edge are all visible from the gutter line. If the roof is older, this is the free look at it. Our guide to <a href="/articles/how-to-assess-roof-age-and-condition/">assessing roof age and condition</a> covers what those signs mean and when they turn urgent.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Gutter guards, honestly</h2>

<p>Guards reduce how often you clean. They do not eliminate it, and the marketing that says otherwise is marketing. Fine debris still gets through most screen and mesh systems, pine needles are especially good at defeating them, and a guard that keeps leaves out while letting them pile on top can shed water right over the front edge in a heavy rain.</p>

<p>They can be a reasonable investment on a two story house under heavy tree cover, where access is the real cost. They are usually not worth it on a single story with light debris. If you do install them, keep inspecting the system, because a guarded gutter that has quietly failed is worse than an unguarded one you knew to check.</p>

<h2>DIY or hire it out</h2>

<p>On a single story house with good ground access, a stable ladder, and a helper, this is a reasonable homeowner job. Work from the ladder rather than the roof, move the ladder rather than reaching, and stay away from the service drop where power enters the house.</p>

<p>Hire it out for a two story, a steep lot, a cut up roof line, or anything where you would be working above a hard surface. Falls from ladders are the most common serious injury in home maintenance, and the money saved is small against the risk. If you would rather hand the whole exterior list to one crew, that is what our <a href="/services/general-maintenance/">general maintenance</a> work covers.</p>

<p>For rental properties, there is a second reason to hire it: documentation. A cleaned gutter with photos in the file is a maintained property. An owner who cannot show the system was serviced has a harder conversation when a water intrusion claim comes up.</p>

<h2>The short version</h2>

<p>Clear the gutters after the leaves finish dropping. Confirm the downspouts flow and discharge several feet away from the house. Look at the fascia while you are there. Put it on the calendar rather than on the list of things you notice during a storm.</p>

<p>It belongs alongside the rest of the seasonal items in our <a href="/articles/annual-property-maintenance-checklist/">annual property maintenance checklist</a>, which is worth a read if you would rather sequence the whole year once than react to it twelve times.</p>

